 ## What you need
 
@@ -61,9 +61,43 @@ needs `libxml2.so.2`), reads and checks the disc, translates the game and
 builds `out/build/linux-arm64-release/pinyon_shift`. Keep the device on its
 charger. Pass `--jobs 4` if the box runs out of memory.
 
+## Or build on an x86-64 PC
+
+The handheld does not need the build tools at all: an x86-64 Ubuntu 24.04
+machine, WSL on Windows included, cross-compiles the game for it. The
+translator runs on the PC, and the game is compiled for aarch64 with the
+pinned LLVM against Ubuntu's arm64 packages. Built against glibc 2.39, it
+runs on ArmadaOS and other newer systems.
+
+Once, enable the arm64 packages and install the cross compiler:
+
+```bash
+sudo dpkg --add-architecture arm64
+# Pin the existing sources to amd64 (add "Architectures: amd64" to each
+# entry in /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ubuntu.sources), then add
+# http://ports.ubuntu.com/ubuntu-ports for arm64 and run apt update.
+sudo apt install g++-14-aarch64-linux-gnu libwayland-bin wayland-protocols pkg-config \
+  libx11-dev:arm64 libx11-xcb-dev:arm64 libxcb1-dev:arm64 libxext-dev:arm64 \
+  libxcursor-dev:arm64 libxi-dev:arm64 libxrandr-dev:arm64 libxss-dev:arm64 \
+  libxfixes-dev:arm64 libxtst-dev:arm64 libxkbcommon-dev:arm64 libwayland-dev:arm64 \
+  libdecor-0-dev:arm64 libegl-dev:arm64 libgl-dev:arm64 libgles-dev:arm64 \
+  libdrm-dev:arm64 libgbm-dev:arm64 libasound2-dev:arm64 libpulse-dev:arm64 \
+  libpipewire-0.3-dev:arm64 libudev-dev:arm64 libdbus-1-dev:arm64
+```
+
+Then build with `--target linux-arm64`:
+
+```bash
+python3 tools/pinyon.py setup --iso ~/forza-horizon.iso --target linux-arm64
+```
+
+The game is in `out/build/linux-arm64-release`. Copy the source tree's
+tracked files, `.local/game` and the programs and libraries of that folder to
+the handheld (for example with `rsync`), then play as below.
+
 ## Play
 
-Leave the box (`exit`) and start the game on the host:
+On the handheld, outside any distrobox, start the game:
 
 ```bash
 cd ~/PinyonShift
